WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.530 --> 00:00:02.750
SPEAKER: One of the first
and most important steps

00:00:02.750 --> 00:00:06.497
to optimizing your costs is
being able to understand them.

00:00:06.497 --> 00:00:08.330
Today, we'll talk about
a few different ways

00:00:08.330 --> 00:00:10.760
to understand your Google
Kubernetes Engine costs,

00:00:10.760 --> 00:00:13.590
and go over a few
best practices.

00:00:13.590 --> 00:00:15.290
Let's go beyond your GKE bill.

00:00:22.377 --> 00:00:23.960
If you haven't watched
the last video,

00:00:23.960 --> 00:00:26.510
I definitely recommend taking
the time to check it out,

00:00:26.510 --> 00:00:29.100
since we talked a bit
about teams and culture.

00:00:29.100 --> 00:00:31.280
These concepts are super
important for being

00:00:31.280 --> 00:00:34.355
able to get a solid
understanding of your costs.

00:00:34.355 --> 00:00:36.230
I'll start by giving an
overview of some ways

00:00:36.230 --> 00:00:39.230
that you can understand more
about your GKE infrastructure

00:00:39.230 --> 00:00:40.730
and applications.

00:00:40.730 --> 00:00:42.770
Then I'll go over how
to turn these details

00:00:42.770 --> 00:00:45.110
into actionable steps,
and then finish up

00:00:45.110 --> 00:00:47.910
with some
cost-optimizing policies.

00:00:47.910 --> 00:00:50.580
Your organization might have
a platform and infrastructure

00:00:50.580 --> 00:00:52.813
team that manages
the clusters, which

00:00:52.813 --> 00:00:55.230
can be completely separate
from the dev team that actually

00:00:55.230 --> 00:00:56.970
write the applications.

00:00:56.970 --> 00:00:59.580
When every team understands
the cost implications

00:00:59.580 --> 00:01:02.700
for the decisions they make,
like how many resources an app

00:01:02.700 --> 00:01:05.220
actually needs, they
can all work together

00:01:05.220 --> 00:01:07.380
to get things optimized.

00:01:07.380 --> 00:01:09.250
So where do you start?

00:01:09.250 --> 00:01:12.210
Well, that's probably going to
be the monitoring dashboard.

00:01:12.210 --> 00:01:14.820
When using GKE, the
cluster's Info page

00:01:14.820 --> 00:01:16.530
shows a lot of details.

00:01:16.530 --> 00:01:19.380
But we're looking for some
details about our metrics,

00:01:19.380 --> 00:01:22.200
so let's head over to
the Monitoring page.

00:01:22.200 --> 00:01:24.450
This is part of Cloud
Operations, which

00:01:24.450 --> 00:01:26.910
has a bunch of helpful
functionality for monitoring,

00:01:26.910 --> 00:01:28.960
logging, and alerting.

00:01:28.960 --> 00:01:31.150
If it's your first time
using Cloud Operations,

00:01:31.150 --> 00:01:33.940
you'll have a workspace created
for you or you can create one

00:01:33.940 --> 00:01:34.990
manually.

00:01:34.990 --> 00:01:36.910
Either way, as long as
your cluster was set up

00:01:36.910 --> 00:01:40.750
with Cloud Operations for GKE,
a dashboard will automatically

00:01:40.750 --> 00:01:43.900
be created, named "GKE."

00:01:43.900 --> 00:01:45.520
This dashboard
gives us the ability

00:01:45.520 --> 00:01:47.140
to see our Kubernetes
information

00:01:47.140 --> 00:01:51.070
over a time series, grouped
by cluster, node, pod,

00:01:51.070 --> 00:01:52.570
and container.

00:01:52.570 --> 00:01:55.240
We're on the Infrastructure
tab, which is a perfect view

00:01:55.240 --> 00:01:58.960
to get more details on the
CPU and memory utilization,

00:01:58.960 --> 00:02:01.840
and if there are any
issues with the nodes.

00:02:01.840 --> 00:02:04.450
The Services tab gives
the same information,

00:02:04.450 --> 00:02:07.000
but from a service-centric
point of view--

00:02:07.000 --> 00:02:09.039
useful when there's
a service disruption.

00:02:09.039 --> 00:02:12.760
There's also a workload tab that
gives you an app-centric view.

00:02:12.760 --> 00:02:15.710
This view is a helpful way to
get an idea of what's going on,

00:02:15.710 --> 00:02:20.840
but we can also get more detail
by using the Metrics Explorer.

00:02:20.840 --> 00:02:23.180
Here, we can create
custom charts with options

00:02:23.180 --> 00:02:25.850
to filter, group, and aggregate.

00:02:25.850 --> 00:02:27.890
For instance, we
can create a chart

00:02:27.890 --> 00:02:31.460
that would let us see the
CPU usage time by container.

00:02:31.460 --> 00:02:33.200
You can even create
a custom dashboard

00:02:33.200 --> 00:02:35.630
that shows more custom charts.

00:02:35.630 --> 00:02:38.480
If you use Cloud Logging and
Cloud Monitoring to provide

00:02:38.480 --> 00:02:41.270
observability into your
apps and infrastructure,

00:02:41.270 --> 00:02:43.250
you're only paying
for what you use.

00:02:43.250 --> 00:02:46.580
However, the more that they log
and the longer you keep those

00:02:46.580 --> 00:02:48.770
logs, the more that
you'll pay for them.

00:02:48.770 --> 00:02:51.990
Plus, the more external and
custom metrics you have,

00:02:51.990 --> 00:02:53.840
the more you'll pay
for those, as well.

00:02:53.840 --> 00:02:55.423
There's a link in
the description that

00:02:55.423 --> 00:02:58.970
gives a great overview and lots
of tips on cost optimization,

00:02:58.970 --> 00:03:02.360
specifically for
logs and monitoring.

00:03:02.360 --> 00:03:05.420
Also, we talked about
multi-tenant clusters before.

00:03:05.420 --> 00:03:07.250
And if you're using
those, you probably

00:03:07.250 --> 00:03:09.410
want to make sure that
you're sending the logs back

00:03:09.410 --> 00:03:11.510
to the tenants for their usage.

00:03:11.510 --> 00:03:15.050
The cost savings tip here
is to use exclusion rules

00:03:15.050 --> 00:03:17.600
so that you can filter out
the logs you don't care about

00:03:17.600 --> 00:03:19.005
before you get charged for them.

00:03:19.005 --> 00:03:20.630
There's another link
in the description

00:03:20.630 --> 00:03:22.500
that goes over setting that up.

00:03:22.500 --> 00:03:25.400
OK, so we've seen the
built-in dashboards,

00:03:25.400 --> 00:03:27.740
but that might not
even be enough.

00:03:27.740 --> 00:03:29.480
If you want to get
even more granular,

00:03:29.480 --> 00:03:32.330
you can enable GKE
Usage Monitoring

00:03:32.330 --> 00:03:34.370
to set up automatic
collection of metrics,

00:03:34.370 --> 00:03:36.390
and then export
then the BigQuery.

00:03:36.390 --> 00:03:40.490
Once in BigQuery, you'll be able
to see specific usage of CPUs,

00:03:40.490 --> 00:03:44.510
GPUs, TPUs, memory, storage,
and even network egress.

00:03:44.510 --> 00:03:46.250
These details can
be used to compare

00:03:46.250 --> 00:03:49.135
actual usage versus
allocated resources,

00:03:49.135 --> 00:03:50.510
and then you can
figure out where

00:03:50.510 --> 00:03:52.650
you might be over-allocated.

00:03:52.650 --> 00:03:55.280
Following the best practice
of splitting teams or apps

00:03:55.280 --> 00:03:57.750
by namespace also
gives you the ability

00:03:57.750 --> 00:04:01.410
to easily see the resource
usage and cost for each one.

00:04:01.410 --> 00:04:03.540
Labels can also be
used for filtering.

00:04:03.540 --> 00:04:06.120
The most important thing
is to consider the culture

00:04:06.120 --> 00:04:08.130
suggestions from
the last episode,

00:04:08.130 --> 00:04:10.110
and help make teams aware--

00:04:10.110 --> 00:04:13.410
and even accountable-- for
the costs that they incur.

00:04:13.410 --> 00:04:15.840
Remember, high cost
doesn't automatically

00:04:15.840 --> 00:04:18.000
mean that something
is wrong, but you

00:04:18.000 --> 00:04:20.519
don't want to pay for things
that you're not using.

00:04:20.519 --> 00:04:22.515
Now that we've gone over
the monitoring side,

00:04:22.515 --> 00:04:24.390
let's take a look at
some ways to take action

00:04:24.390 --> 00:04:26.190
on those insights.

00:04:26.190 --> 00:04:29.490
With the ability to see exactly
how resources are being used,

00:04:29.490 --> 00:04:32.640
you may also want to use
Kubernetes Resource Quotas.

00:04:32.640 --> 00:04:35.070
Resource Quotas let you
cap the amount of resources

00:04:35.070 --> 00:04:37.890
that a namespace can use,
which is another great reason

00:04:37.890 --> 00:04:41.310
to use multi-tenant clusters
to split up by namespaces.

00:04:41.310 --> 00:04:42.960
This can help you
keeping any tenant

00:04:42.960 --> 00:04:45.270
from consuming too many
resources, which might then

00:04:45.270 --> 00:04:47.160
trigger auto-scaling.

00:04:47.160 --> 00:04:50.550
Limit ranges are another way to
enforce resource limitations,

00:04:50.550 --> 00:04:53.370
but they're actually at
a pod and cluster level.

00:04:53.370 --> 00:04:56.550
The real value comes from
understanding detailed metrics

00:04:56.550 --> 00:04:59.665
and using those to set
appropriate quotas.

00:04:59.665 --> 00:05:01.290
If you're using a
version of Kubernetes

00:05:01.290 --> 00:05:03.240
that supports a
metrics server, there's

00:05:03.240 --> 00:05:04.650
some good things to know.

00:05:04.650 --> 00:05:07.350
The metric server helps
collect and then expose metrics

00:05:07.350 --> 00:05:09.780
to the Kubernetes metrics API.

00:05:09.780 --> 00:05:12.030
When an application is
using a lot of resources,

00:05:12.030 --> 00:05:14.070
the metrics server can
be used to figure out

00:05:14.070 --> 00:05:15.900
when you should auto-scale.

00:05:15.900 --> 00:05:17.820
That's great, but
the metrics server

00:05:17.820 --> 00:05:20.520
is still a deployment itself,
which means that it actually

00:05:20.520 --> 00:05:22.320
needs to be running somewhere.

00:05:22.320 --> 00:05:24.870
As a cluster gets more
nodes and deployments,

00:05:24.870 --> 00:05:27.450
the metric server needs
to grow along with it.

00:05:27.450 --> 00:05:29.700
However, when it's time for
the metric server to grow,

00:05:29.700 --> 00:05:31.410
it has to be restarted.

00:05:31.410 --> 00:05:33.270
If your cluster is
changing quickly,

00:05:33.270 --> 00:05:35.910
this could mean a lot of
restarts, which could then

00:05:35.910 --> 00:05:38.040
cause a small delay
in auto-scalers,

00:05:38.040 --> 00:05:40.230
and even small delays
in auto-scaling

00:05:40.230 --> 00:05:42.850
can add up to big impact.

00:05:42.850 --> 00:05:44.910
So the best practice
here is to make sure you

00:05:44.910 --> 00:05:47.100
work with your metric server.

00:05:47.100 --> 00:05:50.160
Newer versions of GKE
support a resize delay

00:05:50.160 --> 00:05:53.070
to help keep your metric server
from restarting as often,

00:05:53.070 --> 00:05:55.980
make sure that your version
supports the scale-down delay

00:05:55.980 --> 00:05:58.800
to avoid too many restarts,
and ensure that you monitor

00:05:58.800 --> 00:06:01.410
the metric server
as a top priority

00:06:01.410 --> 00:06:04.620
to avoid potential
auto-scaling issues.

00:06:04.620 --> 00:06:06.307
Another way to avoid
potential issues

00:06:06.307 --> 00:06:07.890
is to make sure that
any configuration

00:06:07.890 --> 00:06:11.130
changes are reviewed
before they get deployed.

00:06:11.130 --> 00:06:13.890
So if a team unexpectedly
updates a config

00:06:13.890 --> 00:06:16.500
with 1,000 replicas,
you can deal with it

00:06:16.500 --> 00:06:18.780
before it spikes your costs.

00:06:18.780 --> 00:06:20.970
The Anthos Policy
Controller is one way

00:06:20.970 --> 00:06:23.490
to help automate this
by checking, auditing,

00:06:23.490 --> 00:06:26.280
and enforcing those policies
that you've created.

00:06:26.280 --> 00:06:29.520
These policies can help you
control security, regulations,

00:06:29.520 --> 00:06:31.200
and custom business
logic that might

00:06:31.200 --> 00:06:34.530
affect your costs or your
application stability.

00:06:34.530 --> 00:06:37.110
In addition to this, you
can use a tool like KPT

00:06:37.110 --> 00:06:40.290
to build this logic into
your CI/CD pipeline,

00:06:40.290 --> 00:06:43.200
so you're automatically
validating any configurations

00:06:43.200 --> 00:06:45.068
through the development cycle.

00:06:45.068 --> 00:06:47.610
It's also a good idea to keep
your eyes on the Recommendation

00:06:47.610 --> 00:06:48.320
Hub.

00:06:48.320 --> 00:06:50.070
All kinds of recommendations
come up here,

00:06:50.070 --> 00:06:51.760
from security to cost.

00:06:51.760 --> 00:06:53.600
These are based on
your usage, so there

00:06:53.600 --> 00:06:55.320
are specific
suggestions for how you

00:06:55.320 --> 00:06:57.030
might be able to make
changes and might

00:06:57.030 --> 00:06:58.470
be able to save money.

00:06:58.470 --> 00:07:00.540
For example, based on
your usage patterns,

00:07:00.540 --> 00:07:02.340
you could optimize
your compute costs

00:07:02.340 --> 00:07:04.560
by purchasing
long-term commitments.

00:07:04.560 --> 00:07:07.230
We'll talk about that
more in the next video.

00:07:07.230 --> 00:07:08.970
The key here is
having visibility

00:07:08.970 --> 00:07:11.940
into what resources your
deployments, namespaces, apps,

00:07:11.940 --> 00:07:15.510
whatever are actually
using, and understanding how

00:07:15.510 --> 00:07:17.490
you can optimize around that.

00:07:17.490 --> 00:07:19.050
On the next video,
we'll talk more

00:07:19.050 --> 00:07:21.600
about optimizing your costs
based on the machines that

00:07:21.600 --> 00:07:23.218
actually run the applications.

00:07:23.218 --> 00:07:25.260
Remember to check out the
link in the description

00:07:25.260 --> 00:07:27.870
for the full guide on
GKE cost optimization.

00:07:27.870 --> 00:07:30.920
[MUSIC PLAYING]

